> [exercises](#work-through-it): the model is worth understanding in its own right.

The yield curve is where rates desks, macro funds and central banks all start: the interest rate
at every maturity, from a month to thirty years, on every trading day. A handful of latent factors
explains nearly all of its movement, and **affine term-structure models** tie those factors to
bond prices through no-arbitrage. Fitting one takes a no-arbitrage pricing ODE, a latent-factor
filter, a covariance factor on a manifold, and a careful likelihood. Each of those is something
skoro was built for:

- the **bond loadings** solve a Riccati ODE, unrolled in the graph, so every sensitivity of every
  yield to every parameter is a derivative skoro already computes;
- the **factor covariance** is a `Tril` factor, updated by retraction, so it never stops being a
  valid covariance;
- the **fit** is Fisher scoring on the model's own bindings, and its information matrix gives the
  standard errors;
- the **daily filter** is the [sliding-window smoother](../example-sliding-window/) with a window of
  one. For a linear-Gaussian model, marginalizing yesterday's factors is exactly the Kalman filter.

## The model

Factors $x_t \in \R^n$ (typically $n = 3$: level, slope and curvature) drive the short rate
$r_t = \delta_0 + \delta_1^\top x_t$. Under the risk-neutral measure $\QQ$ they follow a
multivariate Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process,

$$
dx_t = \Kq(\thq - x_t)\,dt + \Sigma\, dW^{\QQ}_t ,
$$

and a zero-coupon bond maturing after $\tau$ years is priced by
$P_t(\tau) = \E^{\QQ}_t\!\left[e^{-\int_t^{t+\tau} r_s\,ds}\right] = \exp\!\big(A(\tau) + B(\tau)^\top x_t\big)$.
The loadings solve the Riccati equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
B'(\tau) &= -\delta_1 - (\Kq)^\top B(\tau), & B(0) &= 0, \\
A'(\tau) &= -\delta_0 + (\Kq\thq)^\top B(\tau) + \tfrac12 \big\lVert \Sigma^\top B(\tau) \big\rVert^2, & A(0) &= 0,
\end{aligned}
$$

so each yield is affine in the factors, $y_t(\tau) = -\big(A(\tau) + B(\tau)^\top x_t\big)/\tau$. Every
right-hand side is a matrix product, a transpose or a squared norm, and each is a differentiable
operation in skoro, so a fixed-step Runge–Kutta integration over maturity is a chain of mapped
nodes.

Day to day, the factors move under the physical measure $\PP$, discretized over a day $\Delta$:

$$
x_t \mid x_{t-1} \sim \calN\!\left(\mu + \Phi\, x_{t-1},\ \Omega\right),
\qquad
\Omega \approx \Sigma\Sigma^\top \Delta ,
$$

and the curve on day $t$, at maturities $\tau_1, \dots, \tau_m$, is observed with pricing error
$y_t \sim \calN\!\left(a + \mathbf B\, x_t,\ R\right)$, where the rows of $a$ and $\mathbf B$ are
$-A(\tau_i)/\tau_i$ and $-B(\tau_i)^\top/\tau_i$.

