## Fitting it

Given parameters $\vartheta = (\parQ, \parP, \Sigma, R)$, the factor path is Gaussian, so there are
two natural estimators:

- **Joint MAP** over $\vartheta$ and the whole path $x_{1:T}$: one large least-squares problem,
  solved exactly as in [the pose example](../example-pose-estimation/#solving-it) and read off the
  `RESIDUAL` bindings. $\Sigma$ moves by retraction on `Tril`.
- **Marginal likelihood** $p(y_{1:T} \mid \vartheta)$, with the path integrated out. The Kalman filter
  below computes it through the prediction-error decomposition. Because the path is exactly
  Gaussian given $\vartheta$, the Laplace approximation of the marginal is exact
  ([exercise 5](#work-through-it)).

In both cases the curvature is the Fisher information, assembled from the terms' `FISHER_ROOT`
bindings ([Fisher scoring](../../concepts/fisher-scoring/)). At the optimum its inverse is the
asymptotic covariance of $\hat\vartheta$, which gives the standard errors a desk will ask for.

> [!TODO] Code: the fit
